WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.860
They gave me credit for communicating.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.170
I understood the questions
without difficulty.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.130
I answered.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.310
I expressed myself without
difficulty, but with mistakes.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.440
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.390
Is it important to speak
a language perfectly?

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.430
Is it important to make essentially no
mistakes or to try to make no mistakes?

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.890
To try to sound like a native speaker?

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.280
I think if you've been
following my videos, you know,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.390
that I don't think it is.

00:00:28.005 --> 00:00:31.244
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:31.244 --> 00:00:32.864
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:33.614 --> 00:00:36.315
The reason I want to talk about
this is because my wife and I

00:00:36.315 --> 00:00:42.705
watched the final of the French Open
tennis tournament a few days ago.

00:00:43.785 --> 00:00:48.525
And it kind of reminded me of how
important it is to focus on communication

00:00:48.735 --> 00:00:53.715
rather than these what I would call
almost futile pursuit of perfection.

00:00:54.135 --> 00:00:55.754
At least futile for most people.

00:00:56.415 --> 00:01:01.995
And so the reason I'm talking about it is
because Novak Djokovic, who is an amazing

00:01:02.025 --> 00:01:03.885
tennis player, just a delight to watch.

00:01:04.425 --> 00:01:09.495
And who's also, uh, a real gentlemen
as are all of those top tennis

00:01:09.495 --> 00:01:16.384
players, Roger Federer, um, Rafael
Nadal, so much respect, so much

00:01:16.384 --> 00:01:18.995
respect for their opponents.

00:01:19.575 --> 00:01:25.425
Um, you know, it's, it's, it's a quality
that it seems very often than in some

00:01:25.425 --> 00:01:28.575
circles has gotten gone out of style.

00:01:28.575 --> 00:01:33.075
You know, win at all costs trash talk
your opponent, uh, this kind of thing.

00:01:33.075 --> 00:01:34.935
And it's, I find that so distressing.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.840
You can compete, but after the
competition is over, then you

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:40.890
should respect your opponent.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.720
And of course that's what Raf, uh,
Raf, of course, and, and Djoko do.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.850
And so the other thing that they
both do and, and as does Serena

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:55.170
Williams, is if they're in front of
a French audience, they will respect

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.060
their audience by speaking French.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.420
Now, of course you have to be able
to speak French and understand French

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:06.105
in order to answer questions and
communicate in French to thousands of

00:02:06.105 --> 00:02:09.555
people in the audience and potentially
millions of people around the world.

00:02:10.604 --> 00:02:12.635
And so I was so impressed with Djoko.

00:02:13.185 --> 00:02:17.385
Uh, and I I've heard him
speak, um, English, obviously

00:02:17.385 --> 00:02:18.795
he's flawless in English.

00:02:19.275 --> 00:02:21.195
Uh, I've heard him speak Italian.

00:02:21.585 --> 00:02:22.995
I presume he speaks Spanish.

00:02:23.025 --> 00:02:27.465
I'm sure he speaks Russian,
but his French is wonderful.

00:02:27.795 --> 00:02:28.815
It's natural.

00:02:29.085 --> 00:02:30.015
It's pleasant.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:34.320
The, the fans at Roland Garros we're
delighted to hear him in French.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.110
I was delighted to hear him in French.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:37.410
I was almost proud of him.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.250
Good for you.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.920
And, but he doesn't
speak without mistakes.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:41.970
He doesn't.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.230
He has what I guess is a
Serbian accent in his French.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.440
He made mistakes with his gender.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:55.090
Uh, you know, he, he said things like,
for example, instead of "ma famille",

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:59.290
he was thanking his family he was,
he decided to thank "mon family".

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:00.300
Doesn't exist.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.600
There's no mon, there's no "family".

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.320
And it's in any case "ma".

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.670
Doesn't matter.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:10.140
However, if, if there were a French
teacher there, if his French teacher

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:15.150
from school, we're gonna, you know,
give him marks for his, uh, talk

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.770
there, she'd probably mark them down.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:23.610
And I know that, uh, like in Canada, where
in the federal bureaucracy, they handout,

00:03:24.015 --> 00:03:27.255
you know, bonuses for being bilingual.

00:03:27.525 --> 00:03:31.945
If someone were to say "mon family" in
their tests, they would be marked down.

00:03:31.975 --> 00:03:35.715
They probably wouldn't get
their, uh, bilingual bonus.

00:03:36.105 --> 00:03:40.935
Uh, and, uh, but yet other people will
not make that mistake, but they can't

00:03:40.935 --> 00:03:46.365
get up in front of thousands of people
and naturally, and comfortably speak

00:03:46.485 --> 00:03:49.125
in a way that they look comfortable.

00:03:49.365 --> 00:03:51.015
The audience is comfortable.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.730
Communication is taking place.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.500
So how many of the students who got
very high marks in French say in

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.420
the Canadian school system and the
English-speaking school system are capable

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.650
of getting up in front of an audience
like Djoko did and speak in French?

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:12.400
And so the communication is
so important, of course Djoko

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:13.440
would like to speak better.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.450
And apparently in a previous, uh,
interview, he said that, uh, you

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.810
know, he's working on his French,
he wants to acquire more words,

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.050
which is the right thing to do by
the way, work on getting more words.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.060
A lot of the grammar will fall into place.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.640
Uh, occasionally review the verbs.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.270
Um, I'd love to have Djoko on link.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.420
I think he'd really improve, but the
point is he would like to improve,

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:43.520
but wherever he is, whatever his level
is, he's not uncomfortable using it.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.820
Now, obviously you have
to be at a minimum level.

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:49.820
You can't be up there and
not understand the question.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.740
And this was when I was in Ukraine, for
example, in Kiev and I was interviewed in

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.590
both Russian and Ukrainian on television.

00:04:58.034 --> 00:05:01.905
Uh, I could understand the
questions, I answered and some people

00:05:01.905 --> 00:05:03.315
pointed out that I made mistakes.

00:05:03.344 --> 00:05:03.645
Sure.

00:05:03.645 --> 00:05:04.455
I made mistakes.

00:05:04.784 --> 00:05:08.655
But I think the fact that I spoke
in both Russian and Ukrainian to a

00:05:08.655 --> 00:05:11.655
television audience, first of all,
there was the audience in the studio

00:05:11.865 --> 00:05:13.664
and also people watching on television.

00:05:14.224 --> 00:05:15.885
They gave me credit for communicating.

00:05:16.065 --> 00:05:18.195
I understood the questions
without difficulty.

00:05:18.315 --> 00:05:22.424
I answered, I expressed myself
without difficulty, but with mistakes.

00:05:22.935 --> 00:05:24.674
And so I think this is important.

00:05:24.674 --> 00:05:28.320
We've had this discussion about pitch
accent and all this other stuff, and

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.180
people can spend their time doing that.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:35.120
But I think the bigger thing is
to, to focus on communicatin.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:39.530
As I've said before, just about everybody
that I have dealt with in English and

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:43.969
I have dealt with non-native speakers
in English in my business career, be

00:05:43.969 --> 00:05:49.640
they Swedes, Germans, uh, Japanese,
uh, you know, French, Chinese, Spanish.

00:05:50.599 --> 00:05:52.400
Most of them speak with an accent.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.510
Most of them speak with mistakes.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.270
And yet they communicate very comfortably.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.710
Everybody feels comfortable.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.700
They feel comfortable because we have
the impression I, as a native speaker,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:07.500
that the person I'm speaking to
understands perfectly what I'm saying.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:12.320
And I understand perfectly what they're
saying, even if they make the, a mistake.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:15.470
So we obviously, we all want to improve.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.460
Uh, and we have to get to a certain level
before we can, you know, be comfortable.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.210
And before people listening to
us can be comfortable, but once

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:29.100
we're at that level, then I think
we have to focus on communicating.

00:06:29.505 --> 00:06:35.475
Rather than again, it's not a matter of
you shouldn't, but those who communicate

00:06:36.405 --> 00:06:40.455
at a less than perfect level should
be given credit for what they can do,

00:06:40.785 --> 00:06:45.315
should give themselves credit for what
they can do, even if they are motivated

00:06:45.375 --> 00:06:47.775
to continue working on getting better.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:52.440
So I just wanted to give kudos
to Djoko and I should add

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:53.910
in, I've seen Rafa do this.

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:58.800
Roger Federer of course is
magnificent in so many languages.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:01.410
I've heard Serena speak very
good french, Serena Williams.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:08.085
Um, So, and, and we should also recognize
that English speakers, we say, wow,

00:07:08.085 --> 00:07:09.825
you know, so-and-so spoke French.

00:07:09.855 --> 00:07:11.075
And if we have...

00:07:11.075 --> 00:07:16.025
like, I know that, uh, whatever
that English, tennis player can't

00:07:16.025 --> 00:07:20.495
remember, Scottish tennis player
spoke Spanish very well, but these

00:07:20.495 --> 00:07:25.515
non-English speaking athletes and
politicians and others, they're always

00:07:25.515 --> 00:07:28.755
speaking English and they're speaking
English well, albeit with mistakes.

00:07:29.085 --> 00:07:31.034
And I'm always impressed.

00:07:31.065 --> 00:07:33.195
Even when it comes to speaking English.

00:07:33.224 --> 00:07:33.974
I'm impressed.

00:07:33.974 --> 00:07:37.604
When I hear people, hockey
players, you name it, American like

00:07:37.635 --> 00:07:40.844
baseball players in the US from the
Dominican Republic or somewhere.

00:07:41.265 --> 00:07:42.554
And they all speak English so well.

00:07:43.185 --> 00:07:45.465
More English speakers
should make the effort.

00:07:46.005 --> 00:07:49.875
To speak those languages and get up in
front of an audience like Djoko did,

00:07:49.875 --> 00:07:51.455
like Serena has done and so forth.

00:07:51.664 --> 00:07:54.255
So I'm full of admiration for
those people, even though they

00:07:54.255 --> 00:07:56.445
speak less than perfectly.

00:07:57.255 --> 00:08:02.174
And I'll leave a couple of videos
that kind of follow the same subject.

00:08:02.924 --> 00:08:03.344
Bye for now.

